WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:05.880
"The Batman" was such a big movie that one
of its biggest villains got left on the cutting

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.790
room floor – no joke.

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:12.570
Matt Reeves' "The Batman" took a long time
to arrive in theaters, but when audiences

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:18.029
finally got to see it, they were met with
a truly intense version of Batman who is so

00:00:18.029 --> 00:00:22.939
consumed by his quest for vengeance that he
barely has time to be Bruce Wayne.

00:00:22.939 --> 00:00:30.050
The film has been a huge success upon release,
raking in $128.5 million in its opening weekend,

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.800
while earning rave reviews from critics.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.280
The film pits the Caped Crusader against a
Zodiac killer-inspired Riddler who wages a

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.260
bloody war on the corrupt members of Gotham's
elite.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:44.410
Riddler's mission also forces Bruce to examine
his parents' legacy, which is called into

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:49.320
question by Riddler's cleansing crusade, a
wrinkle that adds a nice personal touch to

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.239
the sprawling detective thriller.

00:00:51.239 --> 00:00:55.171
Thankfully, Batman and the Gotham City Police
Department eventually catch the villain, and

00:00:55.171 --> 00:00:58.039
lock him up in Arkham Asylum for his crimes.

00:00:58.039 --> 00:01:02.600
But remember, this is a comic book movie,
so we're treated to some obligatory sequel

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:07.600
bait in Arkham when the Riddler meets a version
of the Joker — played by "American Animals"

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.690
and "Eternals" star Barry Keoghan.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:15.690
We don't see much of the Clown Prince of Crime,
but he does befriend his new asylum-mate.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:19.870
Reeves previously revealed that Keoghan shot
footage with Pattinson's Dark Knight, leading

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:24.920
some fans to decry the fact that these frames
were obviously left on the cutting room floor.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:30.540
Now the infamous deleted scene has finally
been released, confirming what we suspected

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.099
about Barry Keoghan's Joker.

00:01:32.099 --> 00:01:36.250
[Laughter]

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:41.400
Although the theatrical cut of "The Batman"
gives fans a brief glimpse of the Joker, it's

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:45.650
very careful not to reveal too much of the
villain — presumably to save the full look

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:47.000
for a sequel.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:51.530
And thanks to the bright light coming in from
the window behind him, his iconic green hair

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:53.040
is barely visible.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:57.850
That said, Keoghan's gleefully sinister demeanor
sells it perfectly.

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:02.950
The deleted scene offers a full look at the
Joker, as it features the Bat visiting him

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:07.880
in Arkham Asylum as he asks the villain for
help unpacking the Riddler's sadistic mind.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:12.970
It's an age-old tactic: Use a serial killer
to find a serial killer.

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:17.480
The whole clip feels like a comic book take
on "The Silence of the Lambs," and confirms

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:21.226
that Batman and Joker have already crossed
paths before.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:26.429
"Almost our anniversary, isn't it?"

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:31.790
Since the movie takes place in Bruce's second
year of crime-fighting, this line suggests

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:36.750
he went up against the Clown in his early
vigilante days — which is pretty impressive.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:39.240
As a director, Reeves is clever.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:43.680
He keeps Keoghan out of focus for most of
the sequence, only revealing the villain's

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.380
horrific smile toward the end of their conversation.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:53.720
It's definitely a unique take on the Joker,
a ghastly visage with full-body scarring.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:59.490
This new version of Batman's nemesis is also
missing chunks of his trademark green hair.

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:02.709
Arkham has clearly not been kind to him.

00:03:02.709 --> 00:03:06.310
Coming up with a fresh take on the Joker was
always going to be difficult for Matt Reeves,

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:11.490
because we've had so many different iterations
of the iconic villain over the years.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:16.310
From Jack Nicholson's sharp-suited mobster
and Heath Ledger's unpredictable anarchist,

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:21.580
to Jared Leto's tattooed gangster and Joaquin
Phoenix's misunderstood comedian.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:26.650
Thankfully, the director has already revealed
his inspiration for the Joker in this grimy

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:28.020
version of Gotham.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:32.599
He told IGN that the villain has a congenital
disease that left him completely disfigured,

00:03:32.599 --> 00:03:36.770
and that he's, quote, "very much out of the
Conrad Veidt sort of mold."

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:41.610
Silent film star Conrad Veidt's performance
in 1928's "The Man Who Laughs" was the original

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:43.450
inspiration for the Joker.

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:44.450
Reeves told the outlet,

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:47.380
"He's got this congenital disease, he can
never stop smiling.

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:52.620
And I was like, 'Well maybe there's something
here,' where it's not something where he fell

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:57.680
in a vat of chemicals or it's not the Nolan
thing where he has these scars and we don't

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.740
know where they came from."

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:06.540
Reeves is clearly a hardcore Batman fan, and
it's great that he wanted to honor the origins

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:08.849
of the villain in his own way.

00:04:08.849 --> 00:04:12.910
"This is very, very personal."

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:17.680
The Joker's startling look also feels reminiscent
of the nightmarish take of the character in

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:23.099
Grant Morrison's "Arkham Asylum: A Serious
House on Serious Earth" — and since there's

00:04:23.099 --> 00:04:29.360
an "Arkham Asylum" series planned for HBO
Max, it would be great if Keoghan's Joker

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:30.699
shows up there too.

